Setting (from the preceding pages): A → B is a ring map in characteristic p. Ã and B̃ = B ⊗_{A,F_A} A are the Frobenius twists, and Λ = Im(B̃ → B) = A·B^p is the subring generated by A and B^p. The previous page proves Theorem 1. When B/A admits a p-basis (x_i), the multiplicative map φ: Ω*_{B̃/Ã} ⊗_{B̃} Λ → H*_DR(B/A), sending dx̃ to [x^{p-1}dx], is an isomorphism. The proof reduces, via a tensor decomposition over the p-basis, to the one-variable case, where φ sends the basis {1, dx̃} to the basis {1, x^{p-1}dx}. The inverse of φ, composed with Z* → H*, is the Cartier operation C̃.

This page states three things.

Corollary 2 characterises C̃: Z*(B/A) → Ω*_{B̃/Ã} ⊗ Λ as the unique map that
- (a) kills exact forms dω;
- (b) is a ring homomorphism (additive, multiplicative, C(1) = 1) that is linear over the coefficient ring;
- (c) sends x^{p-1}dx to dx̃ ⊗ 1.
Existence comes from the definition; uniqueness comes from Corollary 1 (Z* = B* ⊕ image of the forms built from x^{p-1}dx).

Corollary 3: for a unit x ∈ B*, write dx/x = x^{-p}(x^{p-1}dx) with x^{-p} ∈ Λ. Semilinearity then gives C̃(dx/x) = x̃^{-1}dx̃ ⊗ 1, i.e. C(dlog x) = dlog x̃.

Special cases. If Im(A → B) ⊂ B^p (e.g. A perfect), then d kills A, so Ω*_{B/A} = Ω*_{B/F_p} and one may take A = F_p. Then Ã = A and B̃ ≅ B. If Frobenius F_B is injective (B reduced), then B̃ ≅ Λ = B^p, and the Cartier isomorphism becomes the classical map C: Z*(B) → Ω*_B, satisfying
- C(f^p ω) = f C(ω);
- C(x^{p-1}dx) = dx;
- C(dx/x) = dx/x.
The next page notes this is Cartier's original setting. The page serves the systematic construction of the relative Cartier isomorphism, which is about to be globalised to X → X^{(p/S)}, in a text on Gauss–Manin connections and Picard–Fuchs equations (the Katz/Grothendieck circle around nilpotent connections and p-curvature).
